text=Remove o Gerenciador de rede gráfico do Gnome '^[[https://askubuntu.com/questions/249944/how-can-i-completely-remove-networkmanager|'''1''']]^'.\\%0a'''sudo apt-get remove --purge network-manager network-manager-gnome network-manager-pptp network-manager-pptp-gnome'''%0a%0aPara o funcionamento do LTSP 4.2, é preciso alguns programas. '^[[https://help.ubuntu.com/community/SettingUpNFSHowTo#Installation_and_Configuration|rpcbind nfs-kernel-server]]^'\\%0a'''sudo apt-get install isc-dhcp-server tftpd-hpa rpcbind nfs-kernel-server'''%0a%0aPra usar o ltsp-utils instalar bibliotecas do perl\\%0a'''sudo apt-get install libwww-perl libhtml-parser-perl libhtml-tagset-perl libhtml-tree-perl liburi-perl'''%0a%0aVamos instalar o ltsp-utils, com ele instalamos e configuramos o LTSP 4.2.\\%0ahttp://ltsp.mirrors.tds.net/pub/ltsp/utils/ ou https://www.idsorocaba.tk/ltsp/utils\\%0a'''wget https://www.idsorocaba.tk/ltsp/utils/ltsp-utils-0.25-0.tgz'''\\%0a'''tar xvf ltsp-utils-0.25-0.tgz'''\\%0a'''cd ltsp-utils'''\\%0a'''sudo sh install.sh'''%0a[@%0aInstalling ltspadmin%0aInstalling ltspcfg%0aInstalling ltspinfo%0a%0aInstallation of ltsp-utils complete%0a@]%0aNo Ubuntu/Debian o pacote '''ltsp-utils''' não é mais oficial, por isso pegar o fonte para instalar.%0a%0a----%0a%0aObter a .iso\\%0ahttp://ltsp.mirrors.tds.net/pub/ltsp/isos/ - ou https://www.idsorocaba.tk/ltsp/isos/%0a%0aBaixar e montar .iso do LTSP4.2\\%0a'''wget https://www.idsorocaba.tk/ltsp/isos/ltsp-4.2u2-0.iso'''\\%0a'''sudo mount -o loop ltsp-4.2u2-0.iso /mnt'''%0a%0a%0aAbra o #ltspadmin\\%0a'''sudo ltspadmin'''%0ahttps://s33.postimg.cc/jxgkbfktr/ltspadmin-min.png%0aNa tela do ltspadmin vamos primeiro em '''Configure the installer options'''%0a%0aEm '''Where to retrieve packages from?''' vamos trocar e colocar:\\%0a'''[=file:///mnt/=]'''%0a%0ana opção abaixo '''In which directory would you like to place the LTSP client tree?\\%0a[/opt/ltsp]'''\\%0aIndicado manter o padrão e pra outras pode deixar em branco e dar enter.%0ahttps://s33.postimg.cc/j7xrytchr/ltspadmin_config_2-min.png%0a%0a%0aPara instalar vamos em '''Install/Update LTSP Packages'''\\%0ahttps://s33.postimg.cc/z7gfidnz3/03-ltspadmin_selec_install-min.png%0a%0aTecle '''A''' para selecionar todos arquivos para instalação%0ahttps://s33.postimg.cc/4q0kqu8bz/04-ltspadmin_selec_install_a_q-min.png%0ae '''Q''' para começar o Download%0ahttps://s33.postimg.cc/rfzpjueov/05-ltspadmin_install_enter-min.png%0aInstalou e tecle '''Enter''' para continuar%0a%0a%0a!! Com LTSP já instalado, falta somente configurar...mas antes vamos editar/verificar se alguns arquivos estão como devem.%0a!!! Configurado Interface de Rede%0aO servidor deve ter duas placas de rede, vamos usar aqui no exemplo a eth1 com ip 192.168.20.1 para o LTSP\\%0aPara isso vamos abrir o:\\%0a'''sudo nano /etc/network/interfaces'''\\%0aE colocar a seguinte configuração na eth1 deste jeito.%0a[@%0aauto eth1%0aiface eth1 inet static%0a        address   192.168.20.1%0a        netmask   255.255.255.0%0a        network   192.168.20.0%0a        broadcast 192.168.20.255%0a@]%0adepois executar...\\%0a'''sudo service networking restart'''\\%0a'''sudo ifconfig eth1 192.168.20.1'''%0a%0a%0a!!! Configurado o Runlevel%0a%0aCriar o arquivo '''inittab''', isso não vai afetar o Ubuntu que usa outro sistema, mas somente ter este arquivo pra continuar a instalação do LTSP4.2, sem mensagem de erros.\\%0a'''sudo su'''\\%0aFicar como root, e ai executar\\%0a'''echo "id:5:initdefault:" > /etc/inittab'''%0a%0aCom isso se cria o arquivo inittab com valor id:5%0a%0a%0a!!! Ver Configuração atual do LTSP%0a%0aPara ver como esta a configuração atual do LTSP\\%0a'''sudo ltspcfg'''\\%0atecle '''S''' e veja se tem tudo instalado\\%0ahttps://s33.postimg.cc/6j82sygcf/06-ltspcfg-show-min.png%0a%0aMaiores informações:\\%0a2.5. Exibindo a configuração atual\\%0ahttp://ltsp.mirrors.tds.net/pub/ltsp/docs/ltsp-4.1-ptbr.html#AEN641%0a%0a%0a%0a!! Configurando os serviços necessários ao LTSP%0a%0aEm '''sudo ltspcfg''' Tecle '''C''' (configure)\\%0ahttps://s33.postimg.cc/7ms750gfz/07-ltspcfg-config-min.png%0a%0aMaiores informações:\\%0a2.3. Configurando os serviços necessários ao LTSP\\%0ahttp://ltsp.mirrors.tds.net/pub/ltsp/docs/ltsp-4.1-ptbr.html#AEN443%0a%0a!!!'''1 - Runlevel'''%0a%0aEntrar e confirmar que esta em runlevel igual a 5%0a%0a!!!'''2 - Interface selection'''%0a%0aEntrar e escrever a interface/placa de rede que LTSP vai usar.\\%0aVamos aqui usar a '''eth1''' que antes já configuramos%0a%0a%0a!!!'''3 - DHCP configuration'''%0a%0aEntre e criei o arquivo dhcpd.conf\\%0aDo you want to build a dhcpd.conf file (y/n) ? '''Y'''%0a%0aem outro Terminal/Aba faça\\%0a'''sudo cp /etc/dhcpd.conf /etc/dhcp/dhcpd.conf'''%0a%0aE agora edite o dhcpd.conf\\%0a'''sudo nano /etc/dhcp/dhcpd.conf'''%0a%0a%0aNa primeiras comentar a linha onde esta:%0a[@%0addns-update-style             ad-hoc;%0a@]%0aficando%0a[@%0a#ddns-update-style             ad-hoc;%0a@]%0a%0ae procure por:%0a[@%0aoption domain-name            "your_domain.org";   # You really should fix this%0a@]%0ae coloque o domínio da sua rede.\\%0aSugestão usar "ltsp"%0a[@%0aoption domain-name            "ltsp";   # You really should fix this%0a@]%0aprocure a linha - subnet 192.168.0.0 netmask 255.255.255.0%0a[@%0a subnet 192.168.0.0 netmask 255.255.255.0 {%0a range 192.168.0.100 192.168.0.199;%0a if substring (option vendor-class-identifier, 0, 9) = "PXEClient" {%0a filename "/lts/2.6.16.1-ltsp-1/pxelinux.0";%0a }%0a else{%0a filename "/lts/vmlinuz-2.6.16.1-ltsp-1";%0a }%0a }%0a@]%0aVerifica se esta com o ip de sua rede configurada na eth1.%0a%0a%0aEm um Terminal/Aba execute: '''ls /tftpboot/lts''' e veja o retorno.%0a[@%0a# ls /tftpboot/lts%0a 2.6.17.3-ltsp-1 vmlinuz-2.6.17.3-ltsp-1%0a@]%0aNo dhcpd.conf criado esta escrito a versão - 2.6.16.1-ltsp-1 - só que ltsp tem instalado a: 2.6.'''17.3'''-ltsp-1.\\%0aMude para '''2.6.17.3-ltsp-1'''.%0a%0a# tail -f /var/log/syslog%0a[@%0aJan 17 20:55:10 Balaio1 in.tftpd[4606]: RRQ from 192.168.20.102 filename /tftpboot/lts/vmlinuz-2.6.17.3-ltsp-1                                                  %0aJan 17 20:55:10 Balaio1 in.tftpd[4606]: sending NAK (1, File not found) to 192.168.20.102 %0a@]%0a%0a!!! Remover no dhcpd.conf o /tftpboot/ se tiver%0a[@filename "/tftpboot/lts/2.....@]\\%0a%0adeixando como:\\%0a[@filename "/lts/2......";@]%0a%0aAgora faça as mudanças no '''option domain-name''', '''subnet''' e no '''filename''' -, o dhcp.conf deve ficar assim.%0a[@%0a# dhcpd.conf%0a%0a# ddns-update-style ad-hoc;%0a%0a option subnet-mask 255.255.255.0;%0a option broadcast-address 192.168.20.255;%0a option routers 192.168.20.1;%0a option domain-name-servers 192.168.20.1;%0a option domain-name "ltsp"; # You really should fix this%0a option option-128 code 128 = string;%0a option option-129 code 129 = text;%0a%0a%0a get-lease-hostnames true;%0a%0a next-server 192.168.20.1;%0a option root-path "192.168.20.1:/opt/ltsp/i386";%0a%0a subnet 192.168.20.0 netmask 255.255.255.0 {%0a range 192.168.20.100 192.168.20.110;%0a if substring (option vendor-class-identifier, 0, 9) = "PXEClient" {%0a filename "/lts/2.6.17.3-ltsp-1/pxelinux.0";%0a }%0a else{%0a filename "/lts/vmlinuz-2.6.17.3-ltsp-1";%0a }%0a }%0a#%0a# If you need to pass parameters on the kernel command line, you can%0a# do it with option-129. In order for Etherboot to look at option-129,%0a# you MUST have option-128 set to a specific value. The value is a%0a# special Etherboot signature of 'e4:45:74:68:00:00'.%0a#%0a# Add these two lines to the host entry that needs kernel parameters%0a#%0a# option option-128 e4:45:74:68:00:00; # NOT a mac address%0a# option option-129 "NIC=ne IO=0x300";%0a#%0a@]%0aFeito tudo...execute.\\%0a'''sudo service isc-dhcp-server restart'''%0a[@%0astop: Unknown instance:%0aisc-dhcp-server start/running, process 10645%0a@]%0adhcp3 ligou corretamente.%0a%0a%0aVoltar ao Terminal/Aba que tem o Menu de Configure%0a%0a!!!'''4 - TFTP configuration'''%0a%0acoloque **Y** (yes ) para ligar/enabled o TFTP%0a%0aEm outro Terminal/Aba antes edite:\\%0a'''sudo nano /etc/default/tftpd-hpa''' '^[[https://help.ubuntu.com/community/TFTP|'''1''']]^'\\%0aE deixe o arquivo '''tftpd-hpa''' deste jeito...%0a[@%0a# /etc/default/tftpd-hpa%0a%0aTFTP_USERNAME="tftp"%0aTFTP_DIRECTORY="/tftpboot"%0aTFTP_ADDRESS="192.168.20.1:69"%0aTFTP_OPTIONS="--secure --listen -r blksize -vvvvvv"%0a@]%0a%0aSalva e restart no serviço:\\%0a%0a'''sudo service tftpd-hpa restart'''%0a[@%0atftpd-hpa stop/waiting%0atftpd-hpa start/running, process 10900%0a@]%0a%0aPara confimar se esta rodando...\\%0a'''sudo service tftpd-hpa status'''\\%0aou\\%0a'''netstat -a | grep tftp'''%0a%0a%0aE pode continuar a instalação.%0a%0a%0a!!! '''5 - Portmapper configuration'''%0a%0acoloque '''Y''' (yes) para ligar/enabled.%0a%0a[@%0aportmap is the daemon that converts RPC Program numbers to DARPA protocol%0aport numbers.  This is needed for NFS and NIS to function properly.  The%0aclients will query the portmapper daemon to figure out which ports to connect%0ato for the various services.%0a%0a%0a%0aThe portmap package is not installed!%0a%0aPlease install it, and then come back here and enable it.%0aPress %3center> to continue..%0a@]%0a%0a%0a%0a!!! '''6 - NFS configuration'''%0acoloque '''Y''' (yes) para ligar/enabled.%0a%0a[@%0aStep 1: Enabling the nfs daemon%0a%0aConfiguring nfsd to start at boot time%0a%0aIt seems this is a Debian based system;%0aportmap is already configured to start, nothing to do.%0aPress %3center> to continue..%0a@]%0a%0a%0a%0a%0a!!! '''7 - XDMCP configuration'''%0a%0asudo apt-get install gdm%0a%0a%0aQuando se entra, se tem a mensagem:%0a[@%0aXdmcp is the protocol used by a display manager to present%0aa login dialog box on the workstation.%0a%0a%0aThe Xdmcp service is not installed!%0a%0aPlease install it, and then come back here and enable it.%0aPress %3center> to continue..%0a@]%0a%0a%0aCrie o arquivo:%0a# nano /etc/gdm/custom.conf%0aCom o seguinte conteúdo.%0a[@%0a# /etc/gdm/custom.conf%0a%0a[daemon]%0aUser=gdm%0aGroup=gdm%0a %0a[security]%0aDisallowTCP=true%0a %0a[xdmcp]%0aEnable=true%0aDisplaysPerHost=2%0aHonorIndirect=false%0aMaxPending=4%0aMaxSessions=16%0aMaxWait=30%0aMaxWaitIndirect=30%0aPingIntervalSeconds=60%0aPort=177%0a %0a[greeter]%0a %0a[chooser]%0aMulticast=false%0a %0a[debug]%0aEnable=false%0a@]%0a%0a'''LightDM'''%0a* https://wiki.ubuntu.com/xdmcp%0a* https://wiki.archlinux.org/index.php/XDMCP#LightDM%0a* https://askubuntu.com/questions/66431/how-do-i-configure-lightdm-to-work-with-xdmcp%0a%0a%0a!!! '''8 - Create /etc/hosts entries'''\\%0a%0aEntrar, confiar com '''Y''' (yes) para criar entrada no /etc/hosts:%0a[@%0aIt is important that several services running on the server%0aare able to map an IP address back to a hostname.  This is%0atypically referred to as "reverse mapping".%0a%0aThere are 2 common ways to achieve this:%0a%0a  1) Entries in /etc/hosts for each workstation.%0a%0a  2) Reverse mapping entries in DNS.%0a%0aIf you have (or will) setup your DNS server to do the proper%0areverse mapping for each workstation, you can skip this%0aconfiguration step.  Otherwise, it is recommended that you add%0aentries to the /etc/hosts file for each workstation.%0a%0aDo you want to add entries to /etc/hosts (y/n) ? Y%0a%0aFinished adding /etc/hosts entries.%0a%0aPress %3center> to continue..%0a@]%0a%0a!!! '''9 - Create /etc/hosts.allow entries'''\\%0a%0aEntrar, confiar com '''Y''' (yes) para criar entrada no /etc/hosts.allow:%0a[@%0aSome services, such as dhcpd, tftpd and portmap use a security feature%0acalled 'tcpwrappers'.  This feature restricts connections from any%0ahost addresses specified in /etc/hosts.deny, and allows connections%0afrom host addresses specified in /etc/hosts.allow.%0a%0altspcfg can add the necessary entries to /etc/hosts.allow for you.%0a%0aDo you want to add entries to /etc/hosts.allow (y/n) ? Y%0a%0aFinished adding /etc/hosts.allow entries.%0a@]%0a%0aEm outros Terminal, vamos editar o arquivo\\%0a'''sudo nano /etc/hosts.allow'''%0a[@%0abootpd:     0.0.0.0%0ain.tftpd:   192.168.20.0%0aportmap:    192.168.20.0%0a@]%0a%0amude para ip que tu tem configurado no seu servidor. No caso aqui: '''192.168.20.1'''%0a[@%0abootpd:     0.0.0.0%0ain.tftpd:   192.168.20.1%0aportmap:    192.168.20.1%0a@]%0aSalve e pronto.%0a%0a%0a!!! '''10 - Create /etc/exports entries'''\\%0a%0aEntrar, confiar com '''Y''' (yes) para criar entrada no /etc/exports:%0a[@%0aTo tell the NFS system which directories to make available, entries%0amust exist in /etc/exports, for each directory.  With each entry, is%0ainformation about which machines are allowed to access the directory,%0aand what permissions they will have.%0a%0altspcfg can add the necessary entries to /etc/exports for you.%0a%0aDo you want to add entries to /etc/exports? (y/n) ? Y%0a%0aFinished adding /etc/exports entries.%0a%0aPress %3center> to continue..%0a@]%0a%0aEm outros Terminal, vamos editar o arquivo:\\%0a'''sudo nano /etc/exports''' - mudando para o ip do servidor. '''192.168.20.1''' Deve ficar assim:%0a[@%0a## LTSP-begin ##%0a#%0a# The lines between 'LTSP-begin' and 'LTSP-end' were added%0a# on: Qua Abr 18 23:57:57 2007, by the ltspcfg configuration tool.%0a# For more information, visit the LTSP homepage%0a# at http://www.LTSP.org%0a#%0a%0a/opt/ltsp                 192.168.20.1/255.255.255.0(ro,no_subtree_check,no_root_squash,sync,no_subtree_check)%0a#/var/opt/ltsp/swapfiles   192.168.20.1/255.255.255.0(rw,no_root_squash,async,no_subtree_check)%0a%0a## LTSP-end ##%0a@]%0a%0a!!! '''11 - Create lts.conf file'''\\%0a%0aEntrar, confirmar com '''Y''' (yes) para criar arquivo lts.conf:%0a[@%0aDo you want to create a default lts.conf file (y/n) ? Y%0a%0aFinished adding /opt/ltsp/i386/etc/lts.conf entries.%0a%0aPress %3center> to continue..%0a@]%0a%0aAbaixo vamos editar algumas configurações que '''ltspcfg''' criou.%0a%0a'''sudo nano /opt/ltsp/i386/etc/lts.conf'''%0a[@%0a[Default]%0a        SERVER             = 192.168.20.1%0a        XSERVER            = auto%0a        X_MOUSE_PROTOCOL   = "PS/2"%0a        X_MOUSE_DEVICE     = "/dev/psaux"%0a        X_MOUSE_RESOLUTION = 400%0a        X_MOUSE_BUTTONS    = 3%0a        USE_XFS            = N%0a        SCREEN_01          = startx%0a@]%0a%0aPronto....reinicie a maquina e faça os teste nos terminais.
