diff:1529094254:1529094254:=1,483d0%0a%3c Remove o Gerenciador de rede gráfico do Gnome '^[[https://askubuntu.com/questions/249944/how-can-i-completely-remove-networkmanager|'''1''']]^'.\\%0a%3c '''sudo apt-get remove --purge network-manager network-manager-gnome network-manager-pptp network-manager-pptp-gnome'''%0a%3c %0a%3c Para o funcionamento do LTSP 4.2, é preciso alguns programas. '^[[https://help.ubuntu.com/community/SettingUpNFSHowTo#Installation_and_Configuration|rpcbind nfs-kernel-server]]^'\\%0a%3c '''sudo apt-get install isc-dhcp-server tftpd-hpa rpcbind nfs-kernel-server'''%0a%3c %0a%3c Pra usar o ltsp-utils instalar bibliotecas do perl\\%0a%3c '''sudo apt-get install libwww-perl libhtml-parser-perl libhtml-tagset-perl libhtml-tree-perl liburi-perl'''%0a%3c %0a%3c Vamos instalar o ltsp-utils, com ele instalamos e configuramos o LTSP 4.2.\\%0a%3c http://ltsp.mirrors.tds.net/pub/ltsp/utils/ ou https://www.idsorocaba.tk/ltsp/utils\\%0a%3c '''wget https://www.idsorocaba.tk/ltsp/utils/ltsp-utils-0.25-0.tgz'''\\%0a%3c '''tar xvf ltsp-utils-0.25-0.tgz'''\\%0a%3c '''cd ltsp-utils'''\\%0a%3c '''sudo sh install.sh'''%0a%3c [@%0a%3c Installing ltspadmin%0a%3c Installing ltspcfg%0a%3c Installing ltspinfo%0a%3c %0a%3c Installation of ltsp-utils complete%0a%3c @]%0a%3c No Ubuntu/Debian o pacote '''ltsp-utils''' não é mais oficial, por isso pegar o fonte para instalar.%0a%3c %0a%3c ----%0a%3c %0a%3c Obter a .iso\\%0a%3c http://ltsp.mirrors.tds.net/pub/ltsp/isos/ - ou https://www.idsorocaba.tk/ltsp/isos/%0a%3c %0a%3c Baixar e montar .iso do LTSP4.2\\%0a%3c '''wget https://www.idsorocaba.tk/ltsp/isos/ltsp-4.2u2-0.iso'''\\%0a%3c '''sudo mount -o loop ltsp-4.2u2-0.iso /mnt'''%0a%3c %0a%3c %0a%3c Abra o #ltspadmin\\%0a%3c '''sudo ltspadmin'''%0a%3c https://s33.postimg.cc/jxgkbfktr/ltspadmin-min.png%0a%3c Na tela do ltspadmin vamos primeiro em '''Configure the installer options'''%0a%3c %0a%3c Em '''Where to retrieve packages from?''' vamos trocar e colocar:\\%0a%3c '''[=file:///mnt/=]'''%0a%3c %0a%3c na opção abaixo '''In which directory would you like to place the LTSP client tree?\\%0a%3c [/opt/ltsp]'''\\%0a%3c Indicado manter o padrão e pra outras pode deixar em branco e dar enter.%0a%3c https://s33.postimg.cc/j7xrytchr/ltspadmin_config_2-min.png%0a%3c %0a%3c %0a%3c Para instalar vamos em '''Install/Update LTSP Packages'''\\%0a%3c https://s33.postimg.cc/z7gfidnz3/03-ltspadmin_selec_install-min.png%0a%3c %0a%3c Tecle '''A''' para selecionar todos arquivos para instalação%0a%3c https://s33.postimg.cc/4q0kqu8bz/04-ltspadmin_selec_install_a_q-min.png%0a%3c e '''Q''' para começar o Download%0a%3c https://s33.postimg.cc/rfzpjueov/05-ltspadmin_install_enter-min.png%0a%3c Instalou e tecle '''Enter''' para continuar%0a%3c %0a%3c %0a%3c !! Com LTSP já instalado, falta somente configurar...mas antes vamos editar/verificar se alguns arquivos estão como devem.%0a%3c !!! Configurado Interface de Rede%0a%3c O servidor deve ter duas placas de rede, vamos usar aqui no exemplo a eth1 com ip 192.168.20.1 para o LTSP\\%0a%3c Para isso vamos abrir o:\\%0a%3c '''sudo nano /etc/network/interfaces'''\\%0a%3c E colocar a seguinte configuração na eth1 deste jeito.%0a%3c [@%0a%3c auto eth1%0a%3c iface eth1 inet static%0a%3c         address   192.168.20.1%0a%3c         netmask   255.255.255.0%0a%3c         network   192.168.20.0%0a%3c         broadcast 192.168.20.255%0a%3c @]%0a%3c depois executar...\\%0a%3c '''sudo service networking restart'''\\%0a%3c '''sudo ifconfig eth1 192.168.20.1'''%0a%3c %0a%3c %0a%3c !!! Configurado o Runlevel%0a%3c %0a%3c Criar o arquivo '''inittab''', isso não vai afetar o Ubuntu que usa outro sistema, mas somente ter este arquivo pra continuar a instalação do LTSP4.2, sem mensagem de erros.\\%0a%3c '''sudo su'''\\%0a%3c Ficar como root, e ai executar\\%0a%3c '''echo "id:5:initdefault:" > /etc/inittab'''%0a%3c %0a%3c Com isso se cria o arquivo inittab com valor id:5%0a%3c %0a%3c %0a%3c !!! Ver Configuração atual do LTSP%0a%3c %0a%3c Para ver como esta a configuração atual do LTSP\\%0a%3c '''sudo ltspcfg'''\\%0a%3c tecle '''S''' e veja se tem tudo instalado\\%0a%3c https://s33.postimg.cc/6j82sygcf/06-ltspcfg-show-min.png%0a%3c %0a%3c Maiores informações:\\%0a%3c 2.5. Exibindo a configuração atual\\%0a%3c http://ltsp.mirrors.tds.net/pub/ltsp/docs/ltsp-4.1-ptbr.html#AEN641%0a%3c %0a%3c %0a%3c %0a%3c !! Configurando os serviços necessários ao LTSP%0a%3c %0a%3c Em '''sudo ltspcfg''' Tecle '''C''' (configure)\\%0a%3c https://s33.postimg.cc/7ms750gfz/07-ltspcfg-config-min.png%0a%3c %0a%3c Maiores informações:\\%0a%3c 2.3. Configurando os serviços necessários ao LTSP\\%0a%3c http://ltsp.mirrors.tds.net/pub/ltsp/docs/ltsp-4.1-ptbr.html#AEN443%0a%3c %0a%3c !!!'''1 - Runlevel'''%0a%3c %0a%3c Entrar e confirmar que esta em runlevel igual a 5%0a%3c %0a%3c !!!'''2 - Interface selection'''%0a%3c %0a%3c Entrar e escrever a interface/placa de rede que LTSP vai usar.\\%0a%3c Vamos aqui usar a '''eth1''' que antes já configuramos%0a%3c %0a%3c %0a%3c !!!'''3 - DHCP configuration'''%0a%3c %0a%3c Entre e criei o arquivo dhcpd.conf\\%0a%3c Do you want to build a dhcpd.conf file (y/n) ? '''Y'''%0a%3c %0a%3c em outro Terminal/Aba faça\\%0a%3c '''sudo cp /etc/dhcpd.conf /etc/dhcp/dhcpd.conf'''%0a%3c %0a%3c E agora edite o dhcpd.conf\\%0a%3c '''sudo nano /etc/dhcp/dhcpd.conf'''%0a%3c %0a%3c %0a%3c Na primeiras comentar a linha onde esta:%0a%3c [@%0a%3c ddns-update-style             ad-hoc;%0a%3c @]%0a%3c ficando%0a%3c [@%0a%3c #ddns-update-style             ad-hoc;%0a%3c @]%0a%3c %0a%3c e procure por:%0a%3c [@%0a%3c option domain-name            "your_domain.org";   # You really should fix this%0a%3c @]%0a%3c e coloque o domínio da sua rede.\\%0a%3c Sugestão usar "ltsp"%0a%3c [@%0a%3c option domain-name            "ltsp";   # You really should fix this%0a%3c @]%0a%3c procure a linha - subnet 192.168.0.0 netmask 255.255.255.0%0a%3c [@%0a%3c  subnet 192.168.0.0 netmask 255.255.255.0 {%0a%3c  range 192.168.0.100 192.168.0.199;%0a%3c  if substring (option vendor-class-identifier, 0, 9) = "PXEClient" {%0a%3c  filename "/lts/2.6.16.1-ltsp-1/pxelinux.0";%0a%3c  }%0a%3c  else{%0a%3c  filename "/lts/vmlinuz-2.6.16.1-ltsp-1";%0a%3c  }%0a%3c  }%0a%3c @]%0a%3c Verifica se esta com o ip de sua rede configurada na eth1.%0a%3c %0a%3c %0a%3c Em um Terminal/Aba execute: '''ls /tftpboot/lts''' e veja o retorno.%0a%3c [@%0a%3c # ls /tftpboot/lts%0a%3c  2.6.17.3-ltsp-1 vmlinuz-2.6.17.3-ltsp-1%0a%3c @]%0a%3c No dhcpd.conf criado esta escrito a versão - 2.6.16.1-ltsp-1 - só que ltsp tem instalado a: 2.6.'''17.3'''-ltsp-1.\\%0a%3c Mude para '''2.6.17.3-ltsp-1'''.%0a%3c %0a%3c # tail -f /var/log/syslog%0a%3c [@%0a%3c Jan 17 20:55:10 Balaio1 in.tftpd[4606]: RRQ from 192.168.20.102 filename /tftpboot/lts/vmlinuz-2.6.17.3-ltsp-1                                                  %0a%3c Jan 17 20:55:10 Balaio1 in.tftpd[4606]: sending NAK (1, File not found) to 192.168.20.102 %0a%3c @]%0a%3c %0a%3c !!! Remover no dhcpd.conf o /tftpboot/ se tiver%0a%3c [@filename "/tftpboot/lts/2.....@]\\%0a%3c %0a%3c deixando como:\\%0a%3c [@filename "/lts/2......";@]%0a%3c %0a%3c Agora faça as mudanças no '''option domain-name''', '''subnet''' e no '''filename''' -, o dhcp.conf deve ficar assim.%0a%3c [@%0a%3c # dhcpd.conf%0a%3c %0a%3c # ddns-update-style ad-hoc;%0a%3c %0a%3c  option subnet-mask 255.255.255.0;%0a%3c  option broadcast-address 192.168.20.255;%0a%3c  option routers 192.168.20.1;%0a%3c  option domain-name-servers 192.168.20.1;%0a%3c  option domain-name "ltsp"; # You really should fix this%0a%3c  option option-128 code 128 = string;%0a%3c  option option-129 code 129 = text;%0a%3c %0a%3c %0a%3c  get-lease-hostnames true;%0a%3c %0a%3c  next-server 192.168.20.1;%0a%3c  option root-path "192.168.20.1:/opt/ltsp/i386";%0a%3c %0a%3c  subnet 192.168.20.0 netmask 255.255.255.0 {%0a%3c  range 192.168.20.100 192.168.20.110;%0a%3c  if substring (option vendor-class-identifier, 0, 9) = "PXEClient" {%0a%3c  filename "/lts/2.6.17.3-ltsp-1/pxelinux.0";%0a%3c  }%0a%3c  else{%0a%3c  filename "/lts/vmlinuz-2.6.17.3-ltsp-1";%0a%3c  }%0a%3c  }%0a%3c #%0a%3c # If you need to pass parameters on the kernel command line, you can%0a%3c # do it with option-129. In order for Etherboot to look at option-129,%0a%3c # you MUST have option-128 set to a specific value. The value is a%0a%3c # special Etherboot signature of 'e4:45:74:68:00:00'.%0a%3c #%0a%3c # Add these two lines to the host entry that needs kernel parameters%0a%3c #%0a%3c # option option-128 e4:45:74:68:00:00; # NOT a mac address%0a%3c # option option-129 "NIC=ne IO=0x300";%0a%3c #%0a%3c @]%0a%3c Feito tudo...execute.\\%0a%3c '''sudo service isc-dhcp-server restart'''%0a%3c [@%0a%3c stop: Unknown instance:%0a%3c isc-dhcp-server start/running, process 10645%0a%3c @]%0a%3c dhcp3 ligou corretamente.%0a%3c %0a%3c %0a%3c Voltar ao Terminal/Aba que tem o Menu de Configure%0a%3c %0a%3c !!!'''4 - TFTP configuration'''%0a%3c %0a%3c coloque **Y** (yes ) para ligar/enabled o TFTP%0a%3c %0a%3c Em outro Terminal/Aba antes edite:\\%0a%3c '''sudo nano /etc/default/tftpd-hpa''' '^[[https://help.ubuntu.com/community/TFTP|'''1''']]^'\\%0a%3c E deixe o arquivo '''tftpd-hpa''' deste jeito...%0a%3c [@%0a%3c # /etc/default/tftpd-hpa%0a%3c %0a%3c TFTP_USERNAME="tftp"%0a%3c TFTP_DIRECTORY="/tftpboot"%0a%3c TFTP_ADDRESS="192.168.20.1:69"%0a%3c TFTP_OPTIONS="--secure --listen -r blksize -vvvvvv"%0a%3c @]%0a%3c %0a%3c Salva e restart no serviço:\\%0a%3c %0a%3c '''sudo service tftpd-hpa restart'''%0a%3c [@%0a%3c tftpd-hpa stop/waiting%0a%3c tftpd-hpa start/running, process 10900%0a%3c @]%0a%3c %0a%3c Para confimar se esta rodando...\\%0a%3c '''sudo service tftpd-hpa status'''\\%0a%3c ou\\%0a%3c '''netstat -a | grep tftp'''%0a%3c %0a%3c %0a%3c E pode continuar a instalação.%0a%3c %0a%3c %0a%3c !!! '''5 - Portmapper configuration'''%0a%3c %0a%3c coloque '''Y''' (yes) para ligar/enabled.%0a%3c %0a%3c [@%0a%3c portmap is the daemon that converts RPC Program numbers to DARPA protocol%0a%3c port numbers.  This is needed for NFS and NIS to function properly.  The%0a%3c clients will query the portmapper daemon to figure out which ports to connect%0a%3c to for the various services.%0a%3c %0a%3c %0a%3c %0a%3c The portmap package is not installed!%0a%3c %0a%3c Please install it, and then come back here and enable it.%0a%3c Press %3center> to continue..%0a%3c @]%0a%3c %0a%3c %0a%3c %0a%3c !!! '''6 - NFS configuration'''%0a%3c coloque '''Y''' (yes) para ligar/enabled.%0a%3c %0a%3c [@%0a%3c Step 1: Enabling the nfs daemon%0a%3c %0a%3c Configuring nfsd to start at boot time%0a%3c %0a%3c It seems this is a Debian based system;%0a%3c portmap is already configured to start, nothing to do.%0a%3c Press %3center> to continue..%0a%3c @]%0a%3c %0a%3c %0a%3c %0a%3c %0a%3c !!! '''7 - XDMCP configuration'''%0a%3c %0a%3c sudo apt-get install gdm%0a%3c %0a%3c %0a%3c Quando se entra, se tem a mensagem:%0a%3c [@%0a%3c Xdmcp is the protocol used by a display manager to present%0a%3c a login dialog box on the workstation.%0a%3c %0a%3c %0a%3c The Xdmcp service is not installed!%0a%3c %0a%3c Please install it, and then come back here and enable it.%0a%3c Press %3center> to continue..%0a%3c @]%0a%3c %0a%3c %0a%3c Crie o arquivo:%0a%3c # nano /etc/gdm/custom.conf%0a%3c Com o seguinte conteúdo.%0a%3c [@%0a%3c # /etc/gdm/custom.conf%0a%3c %0a%3c [daemon]%0a%3c User=gdm%0a%3c Group=gdm%0a%3c  %0a%3c [security]%0a%3c DisallowTCP=true%0a%3c  %0a%3c [xdmcp]%0a%3c Enable=true%0a%3c DisplaysPerHost=2%0a%3c HonorIndirect=false%0a%3c MaxPending=4%0a%3c MaxSessions=16%0a%3c MaxWait=30%0a%3c MaxWaitIndirect=30%0a%3c PingIntervalSeconds=60%0a%3c Port=177%0a%3c  %0a%3c [greeter]%0a%3c  %0a%3c [chooser]%0a%3c Multicast=false%0a%3c  %0a%3c [debug]%0a%3c Enable=false%0a%3c @]%0a%3c %0a%3c '''LightDM'''%0a%3c * https://wiki.ubuntu.com/xdmcp%0a%3c * https://wiki.archlinux.org/index.php/XDMCP#LightDM%0a%3c * https://askubuntu.com/questions/66431/how-do-i-configure-lightdm-to-work-with-xdmcp%0a%3c %0a%3c %0a%3c !!! '''8 - Create /etc/hosts entries'''\\%0a%3c %0a%3c Entrar, confiar com '''Y''' (yes) para criar entrada no /etc/hosts:%0a%3c [@%0a%3c It is important that several services running on the server%0a%3c are able to map an IP address back to a hostname.  This is%0a%3c typically referred to as "reverse mapping".%0a%3c %0a%3c There are 2 common ways to achieve this:%0a%3c %0a%3c   1) Entries in /etc/hosts for each workstation.%0a%3c %0a%3c   2) Reverse mapping entries in DNS.%0a%3c %0a%3c If you have (or will) setup your DNS server to do the proper%0a%3c reverse mapping for each workstation, you can skip this%0a%3c configuration step.  Otherwise, it is recommended that you add%0a%3c entries to the /etc/hosts file for each workstation.%0a%3c %0a%3c Do you want to add entries to /etc/hosts (y/n) ? Y%0a%3c %0a%3c Finished adding /etc/hosts entries.%0a%3c %0a%3c Press %3center> to continue..%0a%3c @]%0a%3c %0a%3c !!! '''9 - Create /etc/hosts.allow entries'''\\%0a%3c %0a%3c Entrar, confiar com '''Y''' (yes) para criar entrada no /etc/hosts.allow:%0a%3c [@%0a%3c Some services, such as dhcpd, tftpd and portmap use a security feature%0a%3c called 'tcpwrappers'.  This feature restricts connections from any%0a%3c host addresses specified in /etc/hosts.deny, and allows connections%0a%3c from host addresses specified in /etc/hosts.allow.%0a%3c %0a%3c ltspcfg can add the necessary entries to /etc/hosts.allow for you.%0a%3c %0a%3c Do you want to add entries to /etc/hosts.allow (y/n) ? Y%0a%3c %0a%3c Finished adding /etc/hosts.allow entries.%0a%3c @]%0a%3c %0a%3c Em outros Terminal, vamos editar o arquivo\\%0a%3c '''sudo nano /etc/hosts.allow'''%0a%3c [@%0a%3c bootpd:     0.0.0.0%0a%3c in.tftpd:   192.168.20.0%0a%3c portmap:    192.168.20.0%0a%3c @]%0a%3c %0a%3c mude para ip que tu tem configurado no seu servidor. No caso aqui: '''192.168.20.1'''%0a%3c [@%0a%3c bootpd:     0.0.0.0%0a%3c in.tftpd:   192.168.20.1%0a%3c portmap:    192.168.20.1%0a%3c @]%0a%3c Salve e pronto.%0a%3c %0a%3c %0a%3c !!! '''10 - Create /etc/exports entries'''\\%0a%3c %0a%3c Entrar, confiar com '''Y''' (yes) para criar entrada no /etc/exports:%0a%3c [@%0a%3c To tell the NFS system which directories to make available, entries%0a%3c must exist in /etc/exports, for each directory.  With each entry, is%0a%3c information about which machines are allowed to access the directory,%0a%3c and what permissions they will have.%0a%3c %0a%3c ltspcfg can add the necessary entries to /etc/exports for you.%0a%3c %0a%3c Do you want to add entries to /etc/exports? (y/n) ? Y%0a%3c %0a%3c Finished adding /etc/exports entries.%0a%3c %0a%3c Press %3center> to continue..%0a%3c @]%0a%3c %0a%3c Em outros Terminal, vamos editar o arquivo:\\%0a%3c '''sudo nano /etc/exports''' - mudando para o ip do servidor. '''192.168.20.1''' Deve ficar assim:%0a%3c [@%0a%3c ## LTSP-begin ##%0a%3c #%0a%3c # The lines between 'LTSP-begin' and 'LTSP-end' were added%0a%3c # on: Qua Abr 18 23:57:57 2007, by the ltspcfg configuration tool.%0a%3c # For more information, visit the LTSP homepage%0a%3c # at http://www.LTSP.org%0a%3c #%0a%3c %0a%3c /opt/ltsp                 192.168.20.1/255.255.255.0(ro,no_subtree_check,no_root_squash,sync,no_subtree_check)%0a%3c #/var/opt/ltsp/swapfiles   192.168.20.1/255.255.255.0(rw,no_root_squash,async,no_subtree_check)%0a%3c %0a%3c ## LTSP-end ##%0a%3c @]%0a%3c %0a%3c !!! '''11 - Create lts.conf file'''\\%0a%3c %0a%3c Entrar, confirmar com '''Y''' (yes) para criar arquivo lts.conf:%0a%3c [@%0a%3c Do you want to create a default lts.conf file (y/n) ? Y%0a%3c %0a%3c Finished adding /opt/ltsp/i386/etc/lts.conf entries.%0a%3c %0a%3c Press %3center> to continue..%0a%3c @]%0a%3c %0a%3c Abaixo vamos editar algumas configurações que '''ltspcfg''' criou.%0a%3c %0a%3c '''sudo nano /opt/ltsp/i386/etc/lts.conf'''%0a%3c [@%0a%3c [Default]%0a%3c         SERVER             = 192.168.20.1%0a%3c         XSERVER            = auto%0a%3c         X_MOUSE_PROTOCOL   = "PS/2"%0a%3c         X_MOUSE_DEVICE     = "/dev/psaux"%0a%3c         X_MOUSE_RESOLUTION = 400%0a%3c         X_MOUSE_BUTTONS    = 3%0a%3c         USE_XFS            = N%0a%3c         SCREEN_01          = startx%0a%3c @]%0a%3c %0a%3c Pronto....reinicie a maquina e faça os teste nos terminais.%0a\ No newline at end of file%0a
